At Tue, 21 Feb 2006 18:10:34 +0100,
Thibault LE MEUR wrote:
> 
> Hi Alsa-Gurus,
> 
> I've found how to patch the snd-usb-audio driver in order to get the
> Audiophile_USB analog capture to work. This device uses different
> synchronization methods whether the the SPDIF input is connected or not.
> 
> This information should be given by the user at driver-loading-time and thus
> I need a new init parameter for the snd-usb-audio module: I know that this
> could be a blocking point, but please read below in order to see how I try
> to make this patch "non-intrusive".
> 
> I've already designed an ugly patch that is working but I'd like to make it
> clean before publishing it. Could you please give me comments and/or advice
> on the following description ?
> 
> This is how I think things _could_ be done:
> * add a new parameter called "device_setup" (integer)
>   - this parameter uses a generic name, so that it could be used by any
> subsequent devices (well..., only if needed of course)
>   - the parameter bits signification depends on the USB_ID for the device
> 
> * if this parameter is not given, the _default_value_0x00_does_nothing_new_
> (returns to previous behaviour)
>   - This will ensure that no setup modification is needed for current happy
> Audiophile_USB users ;-)
> 
> * use constants to name each significant bit of this parameter
>   - "#defines" could go to "usbquirks.h" (for instance "#define
> AUDIOPHILE_SET_DIGITAL_INPUT 0x04"). Or is there a better include file?
>   - Are they any naming convention for these device specific constants?
> 
> * I am currently only using the least significant 8bits of the "int" value,
> do you think an 'int' is enough to cover _would-be_ future needs? (I know
> that this question seems stupid since no one but me has asked for such a
> parameter ;-) but anyway I think it is worth asking)
> 
> * for Audiophile_USB, I need to patch "parse-audio-endpoints":
>   - I have seen a "case USB_ID" section with existing quirks and would
> integrate my patch in the already existing Audiophile_USB subsection
> 
> Is everything described above sounds good?

Reading the above, I feel creating a new driver would be cleaner
rather than adding tons of workarounds for special cases.  It could
use most of functions in usb*.c.

I don't know what change you need in parse_audio_endpoints(), but if
it's complicated, it'd be better to make a change more generic.
For example, we can add a hook as the argument to this function.  It's
a function pointer which is called in the place where many USB_ID
checks are for overriding with device-speicific values.

Just my $0.02.

Takashi


